Root cause: internal/css/parser.go tracks quotes with 'if c == quote { quote = 0 }' and no newline check, in the rule-body scanner (parseRule :1248-1265), the selector scanner (:1224-1236) and the statement scanner (parseBlockWithRules :1375-1379). An open string therefore runs to end of input, the whole remainder becomes one declaration value, and the rule is emitted. Measured, on main, parsing '.q::before { content: """;
 font-size: 48px;
 color: #3498db;
}
.q { font: 16px monospace; color: #000; }': one rule, SelectorStrs [.q::before], 1 declaration, content = """";
 font-size: 48px;
 color: #3498db;
}
.q { font: 16px monospace; color: #000; }" (83 bytes) - and the .q rule is absent from the stylesheet entirely. CSS Syntax Level 3 4.3.7 makes an unterminated string a bad-string at the newline, and 5.4.4 drops the declaration, leaving the rest of the sheet live. An earlier attribution of this defect to internal/css/token.go:201 readString is falsified and withdrawn: grep for NewTokenizer in internal/ returns only css/value.go:114 (single-value lexing) and engine/limits.go:181 (token counting), neither of which is on the stylesheet parse path, so readString cannot swallow a sheet. It carries the same missing newline check, and so do stripComments (:131), splitDeclarations and the at-rule/nested-block scanners (:1314-1355, :1408-1420); readString and the value-level ';' splitter are deliberately out of scope here - readString feeds the admission limit above, which TASK-040 owns, and the value splitter is what keeps wrapped base64 data URIs intact. grep -rn 'bad-string' internal/ returns nothing. Minimal repro, one rule 'content: """;' on a .q::before: Goosie paints '"""; font-size: 48px; color: #3498db; } .q { font: 16px monospace; color: #000; }' in 48px blue text before the paragraph. Measured at a matched 1280x1024 canvas on both sides: Goosie 2,845 ink px vs Chromium 523 - 5.4x the reference, all of it stylesheet source. (Goosie's default -screenshot window is 2880x1800 and paints 8,424, which is why the canvas is pinned.) Chromium computes content: none for the same pseudo. The .q rule after the bad string never applies either - 'Hello world' shows in the default serif - so the whole remainder of the sheet is lost, not just one declaration. The corpus fixture already has it: testdata/render/selectors/03-pseudo-elements.html:47 and :53 both read 'content: """;', present since 2bea5cc which added the fixture set. At 800x2400 Goosie paints 68,830 ink px with the string broken and 49,295 after replacing it with 'content: "\201C"' (Chromium 59,068), i.e. ~9.8k px of raw CSS on the page. Invisible to every current gate for two independent reasons: at the scored 800x600 viewport the broken and fixed renders have byte-identical ink (28,719 px each) because the leak is below the fold, and score_regions builds its bands from the Chromium mask only, so Goosie ink where Chromium has none is never examined. Pre-existing, not a TASK-033 regression: git diff 4de0fca HEAD -- internal/css/token.go is empty. CLOSED f5cec79 (merged 6e2ebfa) as BR-186. Fix: closesStringAt ends the string at the newline in both parseRule scanners, and parseBlockWithRules drops only the statement whose scan saw a bad-string (both the ';' append site and the trailing append site). Proof of the new state, all measured on this host 2026-10-26 and recorded in docs/tasks/TASK-041-CSS-BAD-STRING-RECOVERY.md §5: on one pinned file at 1280x1024 the repro goes 2,963 ink px -> 333 against Chromium 544, and the ink bounding box returns from rows 18-33 cols 9-595 to rows 19-29 cols 8-112 inside Chromium's rows 19-30 cols 8-113, i.e. the leak is gone and the surviving rule applies; the new fixture testdata/render/css-features/08-bad-string-recovery.html puts the leak above the fold and scores 85.50% with 3 blank bands (worst_iou 0.0, coverage 0.075) before the fix and 96.79% with 0 bands (worst_iou 0.508, coverage 0.954) after, so the gate is no longer blind to it; make parity 81/81 at 96.09% average. Oracles: test/css/bad_string_test.go, 6 tests, 4 red before the change, 2 controls (closed empty string, EOF string) green before and after; mustNotLeakCSS asserts on every test that no declaration value may contain '{', '}' or a newline. Four mutations each kill one check - and mutation (iii) killed NOTHING on the first run, which exposed that no input reached the trailing append site; the missing sixth test (a block with no final semicolon, the common malformed shape, expectations measured on Chromium) kills that check alone. Limitations disclosed rather than hidden: (1) end-of-input is still lenient because Chromium is lenient there (TASK-041 §2.1 cases b and d, measured content '""' and a dropped trailing rule), so this is a deliberate divergence from a strict reading of §4.3.7, not an oversight; (2) the same missing newline check still sits in stripComments (parser.go:124) and token.go:201 readString, left alone because readString feeds the CSS admission counter that TASK-040 owns; (3) prevalence on live pages is still UNMEASURED - measured instead over the on-disk corpus with a real CSS-aware scanner (112 files, 101 CSS chunks): exactly 2 unterminated strings, both in selectors/03 (:47, :53), now escaped to \201C/\201D; (4) selectors/03's own metrics were re-measured across all four engine x fixture combinations at 800x600 and are byte-identical (28,719 px, same three bands 457-475/539-554/579-599), which is why testdata/render-known-gaps.json was NOT touched - the 3-band baseline is re-confirmed, not carried over.
